WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.150
"A Testimonial from Dr. Ornish's
Alzheimer's Progression Reversal Study"

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.320
On Friday,
I released a video

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.120
profiling Dr. Dean Ornish’s
landmark new study

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.480
in the leading peer-reviewed

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.160
Alzheimer’s translational
research journal,

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.800
a randomized controlled trial

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.520
showing that a plant-based
diet and lifestyle program

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.600
may significantly improve
cognition and function

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.280
after 20 weeks in many patients
with early-stage Alzheimer's disease.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.760
About 70% remained stable
or actually improved

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.800
in the plant-based
intervention group,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.080
whereas,
in the control group,

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:42.200
about 70% got worse and
not a single person got better.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.200
But no matter how
significant the results,

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.640
the dry statistics just don’t
convey the human impact.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.640
Many patients who
experienced improvement

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.840
reported regaining cognition
and function they had lost,

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.160
but how does that actually
translate into real life?

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.640
Well, for example, several
patients in the intervention group

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.000
reported that they had been unable
to read a book or watch a movie

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.280
because they kept forgetting what
they just read or watched

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.440
and had to keep starting over,

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.120
but after the plant-based diet
and lifestyle changes,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.680
they got better,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.200
such that now they were able
to read and watch shows again.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.120
One individual reported that
it used to take him weeks

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.240
to ﬁnish reading a book,
but after participating in the study,

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.200
he was able to do it
in a matter of days.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.280
Another participant,
a former business executive,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.600
reported regaining the ability to manage
his own ﬁnances and investments.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.640
“It was so much
a part of my life”, he said,

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.520
“who I am, and who I was—

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.520
it was hard saying that
part of me was just gone.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.560
But now I’m back to
reconciling our ﬁnances monthly;

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.040
I keep up to date
on our investments.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:55.600
A lot of self-worth
comes back.”

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.040
Another one said that for ﬁve
years she had been unable

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.360
to prepare their family
business’s ﬁnancial reports,

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.720
but now she is able
to do so accurately.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.240
“A deep sense of
identity is returning.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.880
It’s given me a
new lease on life,

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:14.760
and yet it’s a familiarity and something
I’ve always prided myself on.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:20.040
I’m coming back like I was prior to the
Alzheimer’s disease being diagnosed.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:26.960
I feel like I’m me again—
an older but better version of me.”

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.880
But even words are hard to
describe such transformations.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.960
One of Dr. Ornish’s study participants
gave us permission to share his story.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:43.040
Dan Jones, a musician at
military events and parades,

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.875
who was diagnosed
with Alzheimer's disease.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.840
"What do you notice that you could
do now that you couldn't do before?"

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.120
"Well, for one thing,

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:59.600
I had a set of about eight tunes
that I played in the parade.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:05.408
And I could just go straight through
them from one into the other.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.040
I played for an hour and
a half without stopping.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.280
I think it was pretty close.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:13.720
They move darn
slow in that parade.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:15.320
I was leaving them
behind walking.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.000
I kept having to stop and
wait for them to catch up."

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.209
"Was that something different?"

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:21.000
"Yeah."

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.160
"And what was?"

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.680
"Well, that I could
remember the order.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:28.960
I could make the transition from
tune to tune without messing up

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.000
and getting confused about
which tune I was playing.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.280
Those were all problems I'd
had when we were playing

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.560
at the graduation ceremony.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:39.400
You know,

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.560
I'd forget which tune
was coming next.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.160
I'd get worried about it,
and I'd start making mistakes.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.566
And this just went
like clockwork.

00:03:48.566 --> 00:03:52.400
A lot of it had to do with practice,
but the tunes we played before,

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.280
I had played hundreds of times.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:59.360
And I was messing up,

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:03.640
and in the parade,
I hardly messed up at all."

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.800
"And you were messing up because
you had a hard time remembering?"

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.800
"Because I had a hard
time remembering.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.760
I'd get confused
about different tunes.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:18.400
It was really, yeah, it was
not a pleasant experience.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.240
It was unpleasant enough that,
like I said,

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.000
I went home and put them down.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.760
And now, there was one
day a couple of weeks ago

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:31.000
when I really had
the drones in tune.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.000
It was in the evening,

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.840
I was out on the
front porch playing,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.160
and it was just so beautiful.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:43.560
I played for 2 hours.
I just stood there and played."

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:45.360
"What are you feeling now?"

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:56.000
"Joy. Just happy.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.240
You know, women always say they're
crying because they're happy.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:02.160
I'm crying because I'm happy.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.360
It was just so nice to
be able to do that again."

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.560
"Did that make you feel
better about yourself too?"

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:10.440
"It did."

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.782
"In what way?"

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.920
"I just felt like I
could do something.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:24.529
I had worth again,
so to speak.

00:05:24.594 --> 00:05:29.640
Something that was important
to me was given back."

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.840
Here’s what the head
of Harvard’s Brain Center

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.840
at Mass General had to say:

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:38.040
Big Pharma has invested
billions in the effort

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.560
to ﬁnd medications
to treat the disease,

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.920
but only two Alzheimer’s drugs have
been approved in the last 20 years—

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:47.920
one of which was already
just pulled off the market,

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.160
and the other is minimally effective,
extremely expensive,

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:53.800
and often has
pesky side effects

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:57.920
such as brain swelling
or bleeding into the brain.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:02.440
In contrast, the intensive lifestyle
changes implemented in this study

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.800
have been shown here to
improve cognition and function,

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:09.080
at the cost of, like,
buying broccoli,

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:14.685
and the only side
effects are positive ones.

